.TH TATAMI.CONF 5 "" "tatami"
.SH NAME
tatami.conf \- configuration of tatami and tatamk, held packages, file rules, repository lists and hooks
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tatami.conf
configures
.BR tatami (8)
and
.BR tatamk (8).
It is optional: if it is missing, empty or unreadable the built\-in defaults are used
and tatami works offline with the local repository
.BR /var/lib/tatami/packages .
A file given with
.B \-\-config
must exist.
.PP
All paths in this manual are relative to the installation root
.RB ( \-\-root ,
default
.BR / ).
They are the defaults of the build; the base directories
.RB ( /etc ,
.B /var/lib/tatami
and
.BR /var/cache/tatami )
can be changed when tatami is compiled.
.SH SYNTAX
The file has one entry per line.
Empty lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
.PP
A setting is
.IB NAME " = " value
with optional blanks around the equals sign; the value may be quoted.
An unknown setting or a malformed line is an error that names the file and the line.
.PP
.B tatami.conf
holds the engine settings only.
Held packages, file rules and repositories each have their own file in
.BR /etc/tatami ,
described below; each of them may be missing.
The former settings
.B ARCH
and
.BR HoldPkg ,
and rule lines, are refused with a message naming the new place.
.SH SETTINGS
.TP
.B REPO_DIRS
Repositories as paths or URLs separated by blanks, used when no
.B repos.conf
exists (see
.BR REPOSITORIES ).
.TP
.B FETCHCMD
The command that downloads a remote file.
.B %URL%
and
.B %DEST%
are replaced; both must appear.
It is run directly, not through a shell, so quoting protects blanks but nothing in a
URL is interpreted.
tatami reads the command's standard error for percentages such as the ones
.B curl \-#
prints and shows its own progress bar instead.
Default:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fBcurl \-f \-L \-# "%URL%" \-o "%DEST%"\fR
.fi
.RE
.IP
It is never used for local paths or
.B file://
repositories.
.TP
.B ALLOW_UNSIGNED
.B yes
or
.B no
(the default).
Whether remote repositories without a trusted key, and unsigned remote data, are accepted; see
.BR TRUSTED KEYS .
With
.B yes
unsigned remote data is accepted (the SHA\-256 checksums of the index are still checked).
.TP
.B BULKHEAD
.B yes
(the default) or
.BR no :
whether
.BR tatamk
builds in a
.BR bwrap (1)
sandbox.
.SH FIXED AT BUILD TIME
The package extension
.RB ( PKG_EXT ,
default
.BR tar.pkg )
and the compression of
.BR tatamk
.RB ( PKG_COMPRESSION ,
default
.BR zst )
are set in
.B build.conf
(or with
.BR "make PKG_EXT=..." )
when tatami is compiled and cannot be changed afterwards.
.B PKG_EXT
or
.B PKG_COMPRESSION
in
.B tatami.conf
is an error, and the environment is ignored by
.BR tatamk ;
only
.B tatamk \-z
overrides the compression, for one build.
An empty
.B PKG_EXT
means
.B tar.\fIcompression\fR
and every compression is accepted.
.SH ARCHITECTURE
There is no setting for it: tatami uses the machine architecture
.RB ( "uname \-m" ).
Packages of any other architecture are refused;
.B noarch
packages always fit.
.BR tatamk
takes the architecture from
.B arch=
in the recipe, which is required.
.SH HELD PACKAGES
.B /etc/tatami/hold
lists installed packages that are never upgraded or removed as a side effect by a
transaction, one name per line.
Empty lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
They are handed to the solver as locked, and
.B tatami upgrade
prints
.B Skipping upgrade for held package: \fIname\fB (\fIold\fB \-> \fInew\fB)
for each one that has a newer version.
A package named in the
.B install
or
.B remove
command itself is not held for that command:
an explicit request wins.
.SH RULES
.B /etc/tatami/upgrade.rules
has one rule per line (empty lines and
.B #
lines are ignored):
.PP
.IB EVENT " " pattern " " ACTION
.I EVENT
is
.B UPGRADE
or
.BR INSTALL ,
.I pattern
a POSIX extended regular expression matched against the path of a package file
relative to the root, without a leading slash (for example
.BR ^etc/.*$ ),
and
.I ACTION
is
.B YES
or
.BR NO .
For each file the last matching rule wins; a file that no rule matches is treated as
.BR YES .
.TP
.B UPGRADE ... NO
Marks a configuration file.
When an upgrade brings a different version of a file that exists,
the new version is installed and the existing file is renamed to
.IB file .old
(an identical file is left alone, an earlier
.B .old
is replaced).
A rollback puts the original back.
.TP
.B INSTALL ... NO
The file is not installed at all.
.PP
The shipped file has one rule:
.PP
.RS
.B UPGRADE ^etc/.*$ NO
.RE
.SH TRUSTED KEYS
The trusted public keys are the files in
.BR /etc/tatami/keys/ :
one Ed25519 public key per file (64 hexadecimal digits, or base64 of the 32 bytes), such
as the
.B .pub
files made by
.BR "tatami keygen" .
To trust a signer, copy its
.B .pub
file there; to stop trusting it, delete the file.
Names starting with a dot, directories and files that are not keys (reported with a
warning) are skipped.
.PP
For every remote
.B repo.solv
and package a
.B .sig
file is fetched and verified; a signature made by
.I any
of the keys is good, anything else aborts.
Local paths and
.B file://
repositories are trusted and never checked.
With no key in the directory remote repositories are refused, unless
.B ALLOW_UNSIGNED = yes
is set in
.BR tatami.conf .
The former
.B PUBKEY
setting is refused with a message that names the directory.
.SH REPOSITORIES
Repositories are listed in
.B /etc/tatami/repos.conf
and in the files
.B /etc/tatami/repos.d/*.conf
(read in alphabetical order), one per line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.IB name " = " url
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I name
is made of letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- ;
it must be unique.
.I url
is one of
.TP
.B /path or file:///path
A local directory of packages, read in place.
If it contains a
.B repo.solv
(written by
.BR "tatami index" )
that index is used, otherwise the packages are scanned.
.TP
.B http://, https://, ftp://
A remote repository.
.B tatami update
downloads its
.B repo.solv
to
.BR /var/lib/tatami/repos/\fIname\fB/ ,
and
.B tatami install
downloads packages to
.BR /var/cache/tatami/packages ,
both with
.BR FETCHCMD ,
and verifies them (see
.BR "TRUSTED KEYS" ).
Publish such a repository by putting the packages and the files made by
.BR tatamk (8)
(the index and the signatures) on a server.
.PP
If the list is empty or the files do not exist, the
.B REPO_DIRS
setting is used, and failing that the local directory
.BR /var/lib/tatami/packages .
Nothing is ever downloaded unless a remote repository is configured explicitly.
When a version is in several repositories, the one listed first wins.
.SH HOOKS
Hook files
.B /etc/tatami/hooks.d/\fIname\fB.hook
say what to run after a transaction.
They have
.I Key = value
lines:
.TP
.B Trigger
A POSIX extended regular expression, matched against the paths the transaction added,
changed or removed (relative to the root; directories end in
.BR / ).
May be repeated; the hook runs if any matches.
.TP
.B Operation
Optional: any of
.BR install ,
.B upgrade
and
.BR remove .
The default is all.
.TP
.B Needs
Optional: a path that must exist in the root for the hook to run.
.TP
.B Exec
The command.
Blanks separate the words, quotes group them, and there is no shell.
.B %ROOT%
is replaced by the installation root, and
.B TATAMI_ROOT
is set.
.PP
A hook runs at most once per transaction, after the commit.
A failing hook is reported but does not undo the transaction; a malformed one is
detected before anything is changed.
The installed example runs
.B ldconfig \-r %ROOT%
when libraries change.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/tatami/tatami.conf
This file.
.TP
.B /etc/tatami/keys/
Trusted public keys.
.TP
.B /etc/tatami/hold
Held packages.
.TP
.B /etc/tatami/upgrade.rules
File rules.
.TP
.B /etc/tatami/repos.conf, /etc/tatami/repos.d/*.conf
Repository lists (definitions only).
.TP
.B /etc/tatami/hooks.d/*.hook
Hooks.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
ALLOW_UNSIGNED = no
.fi
.PP
in
.BR /etc/tatami/keys/author.pub :
.PP
.nf
05ad836299e2a530bbffd0b4da78f6bc88189250b32c654c4ef8db13ea09e3be
.fi
.PP
in
.BR /etc/tatami/hold :
.PP
.nf
linux
glibc
.fi
.PP
in
.BR /etc/tatami/upgrade.rules :
.PP
.nf
UPGRADE     ^etc/.*$     NO
.fi
.PP
and in
.BR /etc/tatami/repos.conf :
.PP
.nf
core  = https://packages.example.org/core
local = /srv/packages
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tatami (8),
.BR tatamk (8)
